# React Native + Expo Project Rules

## TypeScript & Type Safety
- Use TypeScript everywhere with strict typing enabled
- Always define explicit types for function parameters, return values, and component props
- Use `interface` for object shapes and `type` for unions, intersections, and computed types
- Avoid `any` - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown, then narrow it
- Enable strict mode in tsconfig.json (strict: true, noImplicitAny: true, etc.)

## Component Architecture
- Prefer functional components with hooks over class components
- Keep components small, modular, and reusable (single responsibility principle)
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks
- Use composition over inheritance
- One component per file, named export matching filename

## Naming Conventions
- Use camelCase for variables, functions, and hooks (e.g., `userData`, `handleSubmit`, `useAuth`)
- Use PascalCase for components and types/interfaces (e.g., `UserProfile`, `NavigationProps`)
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants (e.g., `API_BASE_URL`, `MAX_RETRIES`)
- Prefix custom hooks with `use` (e.g., `useAuth`, `useNavigation`)
- Prefix boolean variables with `is`, `has`, `should`, `can` (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`)

## File Organization
- Organize code into `app/` or `src/` directory structure:
  - `screens/` - Screen components (one per route)
  - `components/` - Reusable UI components
  - `hooks/` - Custom React hooks
  - `services/` - API calls, external services, business logic
  - `navigation/` - Navigation configuration and types
  - `types/` - TypeScript type definitions
  - `utils/` - Utility functions and helpers
  - `constants/` - App-wide constants
  - `assets/` - Images, fonts, etc.

## Navigation
- Keep navigation logic centralized in `navigation/` directory
- Define route names as constants to avoid duplication
- Use TypeScript for navigation types (React Navigation type safety)
- Avoid hardcoding route names - use constants or enums
- Keep navigation structure flat when possible, use nested navigators only when necessary

## React Native & Expo Best Practices
- Use Expo managed workflow features when possible
- Prefer Expo APIs over native modules when available
- Use `StyleSheet.create()` for styles instead of inline styles
- Extract complex styles to separate files or use styled-components if needed
- Use `Platform.OS` for platform-specific code
- Handle safe areas with `react-native-safe-area-context`
- Optimize images with Expo's Image component and use appropriate formats
- Use `expo-constants` for app configuration
- Handle errors gracefully with try-catch and error boundaries

## Code Quality
- Write self-documenting code with clear variable and function names
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions and public APIs
- Keep functions pure when possible (no side effects)
- Use early returns to reduce nesting
- Extract magic numbers and strings into named constants
- Avoid deep nesting (max 3-4 levels)

## Performance
- Use `React.memo()` for expensive components that re-render frequently
- Use `useMemo()` and `useCallback()` judiciously (not everywhere)
- Lazy load screens and heavy components when appropriate
- Optimize list rendering with `FlatList` or `SectionList` instead of `ScrollView` with `map()`
- Avoid creating functions/objects inside render methods

## State Management
- Use React hooks (`useState`, `useReducer`) for local component state
- Use Context API for shared state that doesn't change frequently
- Consider state management libraries (Zustand, Redux Toolkit) for complex global state
- Keep state as close to where it's used as possible

## Testing & Error Handling
- Write unit tests for utility functions and custom hooks
- Use error boundaries for component error handling
- Validate user input and API responses
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Log errors appropriately (use Sentry or similar in production)

## Accessibility
- Add `accessibilityLabel` and `accessibilityHint` to interactive elements
- Ensure sufficient color contrast
- Support screen readers with proper accessibility props
- Test with accessibility tools

## Git & Code Review
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Review code for type safety, performance, and maintainability
